# THE ACID TEST: four GPCs in a GNC OPS 1 redundant set, a fifth computer
# alongside them in OPS 0 on CRT3.  Run from discretePanel/:
#
#   python3 simulatePASS.py --gpcs 1-5 --crts 3 \
#       --tape ~/workspace/pass-run/OI340700-v44boot.mmv \
#       --script examples/4gpc-ops1-plus-gpc5.script --duration 1800
#
# WHY THIS CONFIGURATION.  It is the orbiter's real ascent arrangement: four
# PASS computers flying as one redundant set, and a fifth machine that is NOT
# part of it, driving its own display.  On the vehicle that fifth machine is
# the Backup Flight System; we have no BFS, so it simply stays in OPS 0, which
# is enough to stand in for one -- it is a fifth computer on every bus, in the
# common set, synchronising, and visibly running something else.
#
# It subsumes the four-GPC three-CRT tests: everything they exercise is here,
# plus a fifth machine.
#
# WHY OPS 0 RATHER THAN SOME OTHER MAJOR FUNCTION.  A GPC that is never given
# an OPS request stays in OPS 0 showing GPC MEMORY, which is unmistakable next
# to ascent displays, and an OPS 0 machine cannot be in a redundant set by
# definition.  DM6OPS counts it as "GPC IN OPS0 ALL MF" (seq 038700AA), so it
# is still in the common set and still synchronising -- which is the point.
#
# It is not the only option.  GPC5 could run SM OPS 201 instead: the NBAT
# writes CZ2B_GRT_GPC_SET directly (ARFDPSCO.hal seq 010300BA), memory
# configuration 4 carries the simplex bit, and so a single `ITEM 1 +4 EXEC`
# then `ITEM 6 +5 EXEC` swaps GPC4 out of S2's target set and GPC5 in.  That
# is a louder display and a heavier test; it is not done here because OPS 0
# costs nothing and proves the same sync behaviour.
#
# ASCENT IS NOT REACHABLE DIRECTLY FROM OPS 0, and this is the part that will
# catch anyone writing this script from memory.  DM6OPS's transition table
# DM6V_TR_TAB (seq 013500AA) gives OPS 0 the word HEX'81403F00' -- bits 1, 8,
# 10, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, which index that same table's rows: 0000, 2201,
# 2301, 2901, 3201, 3202, 3401, 3402, 1901.  In words, from OPS 0 you may go
# to GNC 201, GNC 301, GNC 901, SM 201/202, SM 401/402 or PL 901 -- and NOT to
# GNC 101, whose row (index 2) is absent.  Row 19, GNC 901, carries
# HEX'C0002000' = bits 1, 2, 19, and bit 2 IS 2101.
#
#     OPS 0  ->  OPS 901  ->  OPS 101
#
# which is how the vehicle does it too: the set sits in G9 before launch and
# goes to OPS 1 at the count.
#
# CRT3 HAS TO BE TAKEN AWAY FROM THE SET, TWICE.  CZ2V_NOM_BUS (CZ2COMMO.hal
# seq 016900BO) gives memory configurations 1 and 9 the identical NBAT row
#
#     1,2,3,4,1,1,2,3,0,1,2,1,2
#
# whose 8th slot -- ITEM 14, CRT3 -- is GPC3.  Left alone, GPC3 would take
# CRT3 the moment either OPS loads, and GPC5 would lose the only display it
# has.  So ITEM 14 is zeroed in BOTH configurations, and IDP3's MAJ FUNC is
# moved off GNC so that the GNC NBAT has no claim on it.
#
# THAT IS STILL NOT ENOUGH, and it was wrong to think it would be -- see the
# GPC/CRT step at the foot of this script.  USA005350's rule is that an
# unassigned display falls to the lowest-numbered GPC IN THAT MAJOR FUNCTION
# (ledger #181), and moving CRT3 to SM does not narrow that field to GPC5:
# EVERY common-set computer is in OPS 0 for SM, so GPC1 wins it.
#
# GPC5 IPLs LAST AND ONTO CRT3.  An IDP is loaded by GPCIPL when IDP LOAD is
# pressed during an IPL, and never afterwards by PASS in an OPS (ledger #197,
# #198) -- so the computer that will own CRT3 is the one that should IPL onto
# it.  That gives IDP3 its background and leaves CRT3 in GPC5's hands from the
# start, instead of having to be taken back later.
#
# RUN AND WATCHED, 2026-09-22.  It comes up: CRT1 and CRT2 on 1011 LAUNCH
# TRAJ 1 under GPC1 and GPC2 -- ascent, which had never been reached here
# before -- four computers in the GNC set, and CRT3 under GPC5 showing 0001
# GPC MEMORY.  Two things were learned on screen and are folded in above:
# zeroing ITEM 14 and moving the MAJ FUNC leaves CRT3 with GPC1, not GPC5, so
# the GPC/CRT command at the foot is required; and a GPC in OPS 0 does drive
# an SM display perfectly well, which is what GPC5 is doing.
#
# Ledger #140 -- five computers and the timing-unit transaction on buses
# 20/22 -- did not bite here or in the five-in-one-set run.

# --- all five GPCs to HALT, the three IDPs on --------------------------------
+0     gpc 1
+0     mode HALT
+0.01  gpc 2
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.01  gpc 3
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.01  gpc 4
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.01  gpc 5
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.02  idppower 1 on
+0.01  idppower 2 on
+0.01  idppower 3 on
# GNC on all three for the IPLs; CRT3 moves to SM further down, once the
# machine that owns it has been IPLed.
+0.01  majfunc 1 gnc
+0.01  majfunc 2 gnc
+0.01  majfunc 3 gnc

# --- each computer in turn, the set first ------------------------------------
+0.01  script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=1 crt=1 idp=1 kb=KB1
+5     script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=2 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
+5     script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=3 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
+5     script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=4 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2

# --- then GPC5, onto CRT3, which loads IDP3 ----------------------------------
+5     kybdsel right 3
+1     script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=5 crt=3 idp=3 kb=KB2
+1     kybdsel right 2

# --- take CRT3 out of G9's NBAT ----------------------------------------------
+11    keys ITEM 1 + 9 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 4 + 0 EXEC

# --- and out of G1's, then the rest of G1's assignments ----------------------
+8     keys ITEM 1 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 2 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 3 + 2 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 4 + 3 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 5 + 4 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 7 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 8 + 2 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 9 + 3 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 0 + 4 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 1 + 0 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 2 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 3 + 2 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 4 + 0 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 8 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 9 + 2 EXEC

# --- CRT3 becomes an SM display, so GNC cannot claim it ----------------------
+8     majfunc 3 sm

# --- OPS 0 -> OPS 901 -> OPS 101, on CRT1, which GPC1 commands ---------------
+21    keys OPS 9 0 1 PRO
+90    keys OPS 1 0 1 PRO

# --- AND ONLY NOW, GIVE CRT3 TO GPC5 ----------------------------------------
# Zeroing CRT3 in both NBATs and moving IDP3's MAJ FUNC to SM is NECESSARY but
# NOT SUFFICIENT, which a run on 2026-09-22 showed on screen: CRT1 and CRT2
# came up on 1011 LAUNCH TRAJ 1 under GPC1 and GPC2, and CRT3 came up under
# GPC1 -- not GPC5 -- showing 0001 GPC MEMORY.
#
# The reason is USA005350's rule as ledger #181 states it: an unassigned
# display goes to the lowest-numbered GPC IN THAT MAJOR FUNCTION.  Putting
# CRT3 on SM does not narrow the field to GPC5, because EVERY common-set
# computer is in OPS 0 for SM -- none of them has an SM major function loaded
# -- so the lowest-numbered of them is GPC1 and it takes the display.  GPC5
# has no special claim on it at all.
#
# (That is also the answer to how one computer showed two different screens
# at once: GPC1 held GNC in OPS 101 for CRT1 and SM in OPS 0 for CRT3.  A GPC
# runs up to three major functions, and a display shows whichever one its own
# MAJ FUNC switch selects.)
#
# So the assignment has to be made explicitly, and AFTER the OPS transition
# rather than before it -- an NBAT loaded by an OPS request would take the
# display straight back.  The command is actioned by whoever commands the CRT
# it is typed on, so it goes to CRT3 itself.  Verified live: GPC1 dropped from
# two display buses to one and CRT3's digit became 5.
#
# AND IT MUST COME AFTER THE LAST MAJ FUNC CHANGE TOO -- a dependency, not an
# accident of ordering, so do not move these lines above the `majfunc 3`
# above.  A GPC/CRT assignment DOES NOT SURVIVE a major-function change:
# moving the switch re-evaluates the display against the NEW major function's
# NBAT and discards what was assigned by hand.  Measured on the running
# vehicle 2026-09-22: with CRT3 under GPC5 on SM, flipping IDP3 to PL handed
# it straight back to GPC1 -- PL's own bus row (memory configuration 6, P9)
#
#     0,0,0,0,0,2,2,0,2,0,0,2,2
#
# leaves its 8th slot, CRT3, unassigned, and every common-set computer is in
# OPS 0 for PL, so GPC1 wins "lowest numbered in that major function" again.
# Re-keying GPC/CRT 5 3 EXEC took it back.  The emulator's display-bus guard
# (ledger #159) showed both handovers without anyone watching the screen.
+30    kybdsel right 3
+2     keys KB2 GPC/CRT 5 3 EXEC
+3     kybdsel right 2
